00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:56.410
is Ground Control.

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.230
And Ground Control was missing
from our previous diagrams

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:02.050
because it was just
like one of those things

00:05:02.050 --> 00:05:05.610
that you thought would go away.

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:08.190
We built it and it was
pretty resourceful.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.420
It was quite scrappy.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.550
But then it turned out to
be like a critical part

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:14.210
of the production stack.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.630
And so I figured I'd
talk about Ground Control

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:20.620
as it relates to the larger
identity problem at Shopify.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.020
So the parts of Ground Control
that are really important

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:26.170
are the initialization of
the Kubernetes namespace,

00:05:26.170 --> 00:05:29.230
the generation of the asymmetric
keypair for encrypted JSON.

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:31.060
That's where developers
store our secrets.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:35.480
You can find that at
GitHub, Shopify, ejson.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.510
And the last piece, the
part that relates to today,

00:05:38.510 --> 00:05:41.210
is the creation of the Google
Cloud Platform service account

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:41.940
identity.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:44.540
So this is like, the
genesis of identity

00:05:44.540 --> 00:05:47.360
for a service at Shopify really
happens at Ground Control.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.840
So some other
controllers that help

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.665
keep the cloud fluffy
at Shopify, these

00:05:54.665 --> 00:05:55.540
are the Cloudbuddies.

00:05:55.540 --> 00:05:57.760
We really like to talk
about the Cloudbuddies,

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:01.660
because it takes away all
of this technical complexity

00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:05.920
and makes it more friendly, and
so these are my favorite ones.

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:08.350
Thanks to David Neal for
bringing the Cloudbuddies

00:06:08.350 --> 00:06:09.700
to life.

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:12.430
NetPolbuddy ensures that our
Kubernetes network policy

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:13.480
objects are in place.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:16.660
RBACbuddy does the same
thing for Kubernetes RBAC.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:20.020
Bucketbuddy really helps us
lifecycle our GCS buckets.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:22.510
And Accountabilibuddy,
my favorite,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:26.758
grants IAM bindings
for GCP APIs and binds

00:06:26.758 --> 00:06:29.050
the service accounts to be
able to access these things.

00:06:33.860 --> 00:06:38.840
Google Service Accounts
are something else, right?

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.370
To use an east coast expression,
they're right out of her.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.090
I'm from the east
coast of Canada.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:47.220
I don't really like to use
$12-words to express myself,

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.300
so you'll find I use a
lot of colorful, concise,

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.710
and expressive
language to do that.

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:55.280
And so, the few
things that I wanted

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:57.530
to highlight about
Google Service Accounts,

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:59.180
and that is to
demystify them, is

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:01.560
that, one, there are two types.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.560
You have the Google
managed service accounts

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:05.970
and you have user-managed
service accounts.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.030
The Google managed service
accounts are the ones

00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:10.280
that you'll find
in your IAM page

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.880
that look like your project
number, which a lot of people

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.660
don't know, at a
Google product project

00:07:17.660 --> 00:07:19.687
dot IAM dot g service
account dot com.

00:07:19.687 --> 00:07:21.395
You can't generate
credentials off these.

00:07:21.395 --> 00:07:25.090
These are the robots that
keep Google's cloud fluffy

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.350
and empower the
ship, as it were.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:32.420
The user-managed service
accounts, the user-managed GSAs

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:33.990
are the part that's hard.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:36.820
And I want to talk
about why they're hard

00:07:36.820 --> 00:07:41.790
and, hopefully, you will come
out understanding that they are

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:44.815
difficult to manage as well.

00:07:44.815 --> 00:07:46.940
So the second part of that
is that the keys are not

00:07:46.940 --> 00:07:48.680
well understood.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:52.580
We have this documentation
in the Cloud Platform

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.860
that says like, hey,
look, security bits.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:58.420
Let's just create this
JSON blob and jam it

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.280
in this Google application
credentials environment

00:08:01.280 --> 00:08:02.185
variable.

00:08:02.185 --> 00:08:02.810
Quick hands up.

00:08:02.810 --> 00:08:04.348
Who can explain to me--

00:08:04.348 --> 00:08:06.140
sorry, audience
participation is definitely

00:08:06.140 --> 00:08:09.110
going to happen today via me
and Aaron and the whole works.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:11.600
So hands up if you
can explain to me

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.820
all the technical
bits that power

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:18.410
Google application credentials.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:20.050
So we've got like
the security PMs.

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:21.970
We have one, and that's it.

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.420
That says all that it
needs to say, does it not?

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.430
It's just a fun security bit
that you throw over the wall

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:31.112
to the security team and
say, have fun, right?

00:08:31.112 --> 00:08:32.820
The last piece that
I wanted to highlight

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:35.570
is that it works, thanks.

00:08:35.570 --> 00:08:37.620
Shopifam has left
infrasec, right?

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:40.120
And we provide a lot of
support to our developers.

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.120
We have over 1,000
developers at Shopify,

00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:44.680
and they don't know how
service accounts work either.

00:08:44.680 --> 00:08:47.220
And so we have to
help them figure out

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:50.520
this maze of credentials and
identity and access management,

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.520
and what it all means.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:57.320
Thirdly, the keys are
unreasonably hard to manage.

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:01.080
And I say that because,
buried in the documentation

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.300
says, in other language,
that you should really,

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.010
really, really not use
user-managed service

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:11.290
accounts unless you have
the following in place.

00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:13.920
And again, hands up if everyone
knows how to fully manage

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.990
a PKI, like very well.

00:09:15.990 --> 00:09:17.490
We have a few again.

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.118
But like, we've all
created a CA, right?

00:09:20.118 --> 00:09:21.660
We've all created
the self-signed CA.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:26.250
But what about like managing
CRLs at scale or OCSP?

00:09:26.250 --> 00:09:28.680
How does that all work and
where does this all fit in?

00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.440
These are core
competencies of a PKI

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:34.320
that's actually required if
you are to effectively manage

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:36.600
thousands of these keys.

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:40.140
And so that's the
really hard bit.

00:09:40.140 --> 00:09:42.240
It's not easy, and
that's what I want

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:44.950
you to come away with today.

00:09:44.950 --> 00:09:47.940
So user-managed GSA
credentials live forever.

00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:51.520
10 years is forever in
cloud years, by the way.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.530
And they're actually
technologies that we're

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:56.130
familiar with already.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:58.610
They're typically,
in the JSON blobs,

00:09:58.610 --> 00:10:04.120
a 2K RSA key pair generated off
the PKI that is your project,

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.920
and the common name
is it's unique ID.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.930
So if you think you can
just like delete things

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:10.110
and use those certs
again to come back,

00:10:10.110 --> 00:10:11.210
it just doesn't work.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:13.750
There's a lot of nuance in
that, a lot of PKI nuance,

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:19.410
and it's not easy.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.680
Shopify found out that
we push the limits

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.170
of a lot of the products
in Google Cloud Platform

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:29.230
because we poke at the bits
that people don't expect us to,

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:31.580
the PMs don't
really expect us to.

00:10:31.580 --> 00:10:34.420
And we found out the
hard way that, when

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:38.320
you reach 1,000 user-managed
GSAs per project,

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:41.660
that IAM doesn't let
you list them anymore.

00:10:41.660 --> 00:10:44.560
And so, given that, it's
like a slight denial

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.272
of service against IAM.

00:10:47.272 --> 00:10:49.720
We got the feedback
that we should probably

00:10:49.720 --> 00:10:52.180
do something else,
and so now we have

00:10:52.180 --> 00:10:55.720
multiple projects which only
hold GSA service account

00:10:55.720 --> 00:10:57.280
credentials.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:58.520
Like, that's wild, right?

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.740
And how do you reason
about these things?

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:05.790
Are we confident that these
credentials aren't misused?

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:08.870
An employee that
had a hot exit, are

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.750
we confident that any keys
didn't go along with them?

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:14.220
We generally do a good job
at auditing these things,

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:16.280
but there are just
a lot of keys that

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.590
have grown with us
through the years

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:23.870
that I would deem as
sensitive, and we'd

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.370
like to do something better.

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.680
We'd like to change.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:31.640
And so, given all of this,
this doesn't include identities

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:34.820
for like the instances or the
nodes or the GKE node pools.

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.920
This is just like
service accounts

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:38.930
that have been created
for applications

00:11:38.930 --> 00:11:40.520
that run on our cloud platform.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:41.930
This is not the rest of them.

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:44.780
These 1,200 so service
accounts that I mentioned

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.060
are just for services.

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:48.740
So we, in fact, have a lot more.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:54.590
So all that to say
is that containers

00:11:54.590 --> 00:11:57.710
that run at Shopify
in our cloud platform

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:03.560
are experiencing what I would
call a conflict of identity.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:05.888
And you know, we've
created these Cloudbuddies

00:12:05.888 --> 00:12:07.430
and our other
communities controllers

00:12:07.430 --> 00:12:09.020
and some other automation.

00:12:09.020 --> 00:12:10.190
We're a software company.

00:12:10.190 --> 00:12:14.960
We create cool things to help
solve our problems for us,

00:12:14.960 --> 00:12:17.090
but scaling does come
with its challenges

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:20.803
like the over 1,000 service
accounts per project thing.

00:12:20.803 --> 00:12:22.470
So the product that
you're about to see,

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.690
the technology that
you're about to see really

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.460
hopes to address a
lot of these problems

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:34.080
and further consolidate the
identities of workloads,

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:36.730
bridging trust
domains, as it were.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:38.900
In Kubernetes, we would
just call this a namespace.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:40.983
If we could just namespace
a series of identities,

00:12:40.983 --> 00:12:41.970
wouldn't that be neat?

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:45.670
But who am I to say?

00:12:45.670 --> 00:12:47.740
So with that, I
like definitions.

00:12:47.740 --> 00:12:50.350
I like these $12-dollar
words and expressions.

00:12:50.350 --> 00:12:52.760
Aaron will give you
a lot more of these,

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:54.280
or a few more definitions.

00:12:54.280 --> 00:12:56.740
I stole one from him,
and it's this one

00:12:56.740 --> 00:12:59.760
because I think it's
really important.

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:02.150
So we do a lot of talking
about workloads today.

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:09.090
Workloads are
everything everywhere.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:12.150
And what I mean by that
is that they are software

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:16.180
running on a computer, they are
containers, they're binaries.

00:13:16.180 --> 00:13:20.650
They're just a thing talking to
a modern authentication system,

00:13:20.650 --> 00:13:21.150
right?

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:25.620
And so workloads at Shopify,
including human workloads,

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:29.970
leverage short-lived
signed JWTs,

00:13:29.970 --> 00:13:36.240
"jots" as it were, for
tools G Cloud, kubectl.

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:38.140
All these tools use
these technologies.

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:42.030
And so the impact here
really is that we interact

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.120
with these
technologies as humans

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.630
more than we probably
realize that we do.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:51.690
It powers
accounts.shopify.com-- you know,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:54.870
that is our identity
application at Shopify--

00:13:54.870 --> 00:13:56.820
every oauth2proxy
that you've ever used,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.730
the bitly oauth2proxy,
BuzzFeed SSO, the whole thing,

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:02.520
and Google.

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:08.640
So it's largely my
opinion that we should be

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:10.888
consolidating these identities.

00:14:10.888 --> 00:14:12.930
We should be consolidating
all these technologies

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:17.658
and using them just
as everybody else is.

00:14:17.658 --> 00:14:19.700
So with that, I'm going
to hand it over to Aaron,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:23.540
and he's going to show
you something new.

00:14:23.540 --> 00:14:26.040
AARON SMALL: Thank you.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:28.985
Let's see if I can
figure this out.

00:14:28.985 --> 00:14:30.610
So as I was getting
ready for this talk

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:32.027
and putting these
slides together,

00:14:32.027 --> 00:14:34.120
a lot of the feedback
I got from my peers

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:36.400
was that I tried to cover
too many complicated topics

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:39.310
in the time that I had and that
I didn't go into enough depth

00:14:39.310 --> 00:14:42.313
on any given one of them to
actually explain it in full.

00:14:42.313 --> 00:14:44.230
And they were right, but
I think that actually

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:45.700
points to a bigger story.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:48.520
And that bigger story is
that identity and credential

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.460
management is hard,
like capital H hard.

00:14:51.460 --> 00:14:52.780
It's a really deep space.

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:54.700
You have to worry about
credential issuance

00:14:54.700 --> 00:14:57.580
and distribution and that long
list that Jonathan listed off

00:14:57.580 --> 00:15:00.280
earlier, and revocation
and auditing and renewal

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:03.040
and exfiltration protection--
that's my first $12-dollar

00:15:03.040 --> 00:15:03.730
word--

00:15:03.730 --> 00:15:07.340
and replay protection,
and on and on and on.

00:15:07.340 --> 00:15:10.480
If you add to that the alphabet
soup of all the standards you

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:15.320
have to deal with, like
OIDC, JWTs, RFC 7518,

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.970
and then you layer
on top of that all

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:19.490
the unique authentication
and authorization systems

00:15:19.490 --> 00:15:22.370
that each cloud provider
implements, I can end up

00:15:22.370 --> 00:15:25.290
talking for like two hours
when all Emily asked was,

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:28.500
how do I access cloud
storage from GKE?

00:15:28.500 --> 00:15:31.200
So I did the work to
simplify the presentation.

00:15:31.200 --> 00:15:35.130
We won't cover my favorite RFCs
and their finer implementation

00:15:35.130 --> 00:15:37.512
details, but more
importantly than that,

00:15:37.512 --> 00:15:38.970
I think we simplified
the practice.

00:15:41.590 --> 00:15:43.870
Jonathan from eastern Canada
addressed these problems

00:15:43.870 --> 00:15:45.610
with some really
heroic engineering

00:15:45.610 --> 00:15:48.040
and actually shouldered
some of Emily's burden,

00:15:48.040 --> 00:15:51.050
and he definitely made
Emily's life easier.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:53.500
I want to take a step back
and look at how this works

00:15:53.500 --> 00:15:55.180
for the rest of
the world though,

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.120
except I'm from a big old
west-coast city by the bay,

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:01.520
and I'm going to use as many
$12-dollar words as I can.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:03.445
So the first thing you
do is you go to IAM

00:16:03.445 --> 00:16:06.210
and you create an
IAM service account.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.160
Then you download its key.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:09.750
And when you download
that key, you

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:13.638
get this lovely little warning
and it's perfectly accurate.

00:16:13.638 --> 00:16:14.680
Like, don't get me wrong.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:18.195
You should read it carefully,
because it's perfectly true.

00:16:18.195 --> 00:16:19.570
And then you have
all these keys.

00:16:19.570 --> 00:16:21.130
And this is actually
a screenshot from one

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:22.270
of my own personal accounts.

00:16:22.270 --> 00:16:23.620
And I have a very good
reason, I assure you,

00:16:23.620 --> 00:16:25.162
for having this many
keys downloaded,

00:16:25.162 --> 00:16:28.460
but I'm not going to
tell you what it is.

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:31.970
Once you have this key, you
run some kubectl commands

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.400
against it and you end up
with a Kubernetes secret.

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.370
And then you can update
your Kubernetes YAML files

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:41.120
with this secret, and
you have to mount it

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:43.990
in places that the Google
Client Libraries know to look.

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:45.470
And that's pretty much it.

00:16:45.470 --> 00:16:49.400
Once you mount this secret
in all the right places,

00:16:49.400 --> 00:16:51.500
your Kubernetes
workloads know which

00:16:51.500 --> 00:16:55.040
identity to use when
talking to Google Cloud.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:56.540
And at first glance,
there's nothing

00:16:56.540 --> 00:16:58.220
really wrong with
this approach, right?

00:16:58.220 --> 00:17:01.760
Like, from a strict threat
modeling perspective,

00:17:01.760 --> 00:17:04.115
as long as there is no
vulnerabilities anywhere

00:17:04.115 --> 00:17:05.990
in your application or
anywhere in Kubernetes

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:09.230
or in any of your dependencies,
it's probably fine.

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.672
Kubernetes handles secrets
in really rational ways.

00:17:11.672 --> 00:17:13.339
It's not going to
accidentally expose it

00:17:13.339 --> 00:17:15.829
to the wrong workload.

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:17.602
However, manageability
gets quite complex.

00:17:17.602 --> 00:17:19.060
If you have thousands
of these keys

00:17:19.060 --> 00:17:20.950
that you're managing
as individual secrets

00:17:20.950 --> 00:17:23.230
in various Kubernetes
clusters, you

00:17:23.230 --> 00:17:26.020
would have to build a
system to manage them.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:29.860
And then, furthermore, if
it does get compromised,

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.230
if someone does manage
to get their hands on one

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:35.890
of these secrets, they
can exfiltrate it.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:37.520
They can take it with them.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:39.970
And once they have
it back home, they

00:17:39.970 --> 00:17:42.460
can use it to perform
whatever malicious action they

00:17:42.460 --> 00:17:45.460
had planned until
either you notice

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:49.420
or until it expires in
10 years, which I guess

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:53.190
is forever in cloud land.

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:56.010
So this all comes down to
this concept of blast radius,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.960
and security professionals
like me always

00:17:57.960 --> 00:17:59.100
talk about blast radius.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:00.930
But what this really
means is, let's first

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:03.980
assume that something's
going to go wrong.

00:18:03.980 --> 00:18:06.440
When this thing goes
wrong, what else breaks?

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.660
And I don't mean what
pieces of your application

00:18:08.660 --> 00:18:11.480
stop working, I mean,
what fundamental security

00:18:11.480 --> 00:18:16.090
assumptions are no longer valid
when something else goes wrong?

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.008
We used to call this
defense in depth.

00:18:18.008 --> 00:18:20.050
Now we called it building
to reduce blast radius.

00:18:20.050 --> 00:18:21.770
It all means roughly
the same thing,

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:24.445
which is making it as hard
as possible for an attacker

00:18:24.445 --> 00:18:27.760
to turn one vulnerability in
one part of your application

00:18:27.760 --> 00:18:31.240
into a successful
attack on another.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:32.410
Brief aside on containers.

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:33.520
I love containers.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:37.330
They help a lot when it comes
to reducing the blast radius.

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:39.770
You can use some really
cool security features.

00:18:39.770 --> 00:18:46.480
You can lock them down to
only access certain kernel--

00:18:46.480 --> 00:18:48.928
what's the word?

00:18:48.928 --> 00:18:49.470
That's right.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:51.803
You can lock them down to
only access certain sys calls.

00:18:51.803 --> 00:18:54.450
You can make their
file systems read only.

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:56.580
The Kubernetes
networking plugins

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:00.630
can provide good
policies to limit access.

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:03.420
Then there's products built
on top of the whole thing,

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:06.210
like Istio, that can provide
great encryption of all

00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:08.190
these inter-container
communication,

00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:10.883
and can provide a
management layer.

00:19:10.883 --> 00:19:12.300
But in real life,
these containers

00:19:12.300 --> 00:19:13.450
have to interact
with each other,

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.837
and they also have to interact
with other cloud services.

00:19:15.837 --> 00:19:17.670
And once you do that,
you find yourself back

00:19:17.670 --> 00:19:23.050
at square one, which was
credential management is hard.

00:19:23.050 --> 00:19:25.660
When I'm building a
cloud-native application,

00:19:25.660 --> 00:19:28.630
I care about the credentials
and I care about blast radius,

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:31.150
but that doesn't mean I want
to manage the individual life

00:19:31.150 --> 00:19:33.340
cycles of every
secret and every key

00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:35.450
that my identity
system relies on.

00:19:35.450 --> 00:19:37.930
I do want to make
these broad security

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:41.350
statements like, this component
can only talk to that thing,

00:19:41.350 --> 00:19:44.350
this cloud service only
has access to this role

00:19:44.350 --> 00:19:46.360
or can call this one API.

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:49.590
The implementation details of
credential lifecycle management

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:51.750
and key lifecycle management
should be automatic,

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:54.670
and it should be managed
by the cloud provider.

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:56.358
I guess what I'm
saying is the best

00:19:56.358 --> 00:19:58.400
way to solve the credential
management problem is

00:19:58.400 --> 00:20:01.640
to get somebody else to do it
for you, especially if they'll

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:05.250
be fool enough to stand up on
stage and volunteer to do it.

00:20:05.250 --> 00:20:07.430
There's so many edge
cases to consider.

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.228
There's so many best practices
to be aware of and to enforce,

00:20:10.228 --> 00:20:12.520
I would want a whole team of
people that were dedicated

00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:15.460
to its design and maintenance.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:17.160
OK, my turn for
audience participation,

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:18.410
although I can barely see you.

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:21.680
So who has used a Kubernetes
service account or an IAM

00:20:21.680 --> 00:20:24.700
service account on
any cloud provider?

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:27.110
All right, I want you
to keep your hands up

00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:31.750
if you've ever built your own
identity system from scratch.

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.090
OK, so there's a couple of you.

00:20:33.090 --> 00:20:34.210
That's awesome.

00:20:34.210 --> 00:20:36.460
Towards the end of the talk,
I'm going to come back up

00:20:36.460 --> 00:20:38.290
and we can nerd out
on implementation.

00:20:38.290 --> 00:20:39.165
Until then, stand by.

00:20:44.932 --> 00:20:46.390
So Shopify was
really, really great

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:48.400
to work with on
this problem space.

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.050
They have, like
Jonathan mentioned,

00:20:50.050 --> 00:20:53.680
this habit of pushing on all
the bits of our service we maybe

00:20:53.680 --> 00:20:56.710
didn't expect to get
pushed on that hard.

00:20:56.710 --> 00:20:58.570
When we talked about
this set of problems,

00:20:58.570 --> 00:21:01.210
their perspective was really
valuable to the eventual design

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:02.260
we landed on.

00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:04.150
We heard from Jonathan
and many others

00:21:04.150 --> 00:21:07.210
that the process of accessing
Google Cloud Services

00:21:07.210 --> 00:21:10.513
from within Kubernetes
Engine was really onerous,

00:21:10.513 --> 00:21:12.430
that managing these
long-lived secrets and all

00:21:12.430 --> 00:21:14.840
these IAM service
accounts was very painful.

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:17.680
And then additionally, as
security professionals,

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:20.950
neither myself nor Jonathan were
happy with all these keys just

00:21:20.950 --> 00:21:24.370
flying around everywhere,
either being unmanaged entirely

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:27.720
or being managed in
a decentralized way.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:29.670
So we decided to take a
crack at the solution,

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:31.340
and this is what
we came up with.

00:21:31.340 --> 00:21:34.590
Today, I'm introducing
Workload Identity.

00:21:34.590 --> 00:21:36.960
It'll be in beta in
a few weeks, and it's

00:21:36.960 --> 00:21:39.120
designed to help people
like Jonathan and Emily,

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:41.162
and also, for all the
security folks in the room,

00:21:41.162 --> 00:21:44.400
to help you sleep a
little better at night.

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.390
Last year, I gave a
big speech about trust

00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:52.540
and how using a cloud provider
is an exercise in trust.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:54.180
I think part of
that relationship

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:57.420
is we need to offer
services for you

00:21:57.420 --> 00:22:00.630
to build on top of that are
built on really strong security

00:22:00.630 --> 00:22:03.910
principles.

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:09.580
So Workload Identity makes it
easy to access Google Cloud

00:22:09.580 --> 00:22:11.560
products from within
Kubernetes Engine

00:22:11.560 --> 00:22:13.330
without becoming an
expert in identity,

00:22:13.330 --> 00:22:15.820
without becoming an expert
in credential management.

00:22:15.820 --> 00:22:19.710
You don't have to manage
any keys or any secrets.

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:22.210
Related to this, we have this
new concept called an identity

00:22:22.210 --> 00:22:24.100
namespace, and I'll go
into exactly what that

00:22:24.100 --> 00:22:25.335
means more in a minute.

00:22:25.335 --> 00:22:26.710
But in short, it's
going to allow

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:28.752
you to define the relationship
between Kubernetes

00:22:28.752 --> 00:22:31.720
workloads and the
Google Cloud identities

00:22:31.720 --> 00:22:33.400
that they should
use without a bunch

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:35.470
of excess micromanagement.

00:22:35.470 --> 00:22:37.130
It's going to work
across clusters,

00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:39.502
and it works with the existing
Google Client Libraries

00:22:39.502 --> 00:22:40.960
that you're probably
already using.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:46.510
I think if you're going to
get someone else to solve

00:22:46.510 --> 00:22:48.340
a problem like this
for you, it's only fair

00:22:48.340 --> 00:22:50.590
that you understand
the kinds of principles

00:22:50.590 --> 00:22:54.360
that led to the design
they ended up with.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.085
I like sharing
principles like this.

00:22:56.085 --> 00:22:57.460
I think it builds
a lot of trust,

00:22:57.460 --> 00:22:58.918
and I think it
helps you understand

00:22:58.918 --> 00:23:00.690
why we made the
trade offs we did,

00:23:00.690 --> 00:23:03.148
and maybe even give you a little
insight into the direction

00:23:03.148 --> 00:23:04.520
that we're going.

00:23:04.520 --> 00:23:06.760
So the first major principle
we designed towards

00:23:06.760 --> 00:23:08.500
was least-privilege.

00:23:08.500 --> 00:23:11.650
All that really means is
not giving any one component

00:23:11.650 --> 00:23:14.020
more permissions than
it actually needs.

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:16.660
In this instance,
it means we can't

00:23:16.660 --> 00:23:21.130
force you to be less stingy
with your permissions

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:22.660
than you want to be.

00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.480
And specifically, that means we
couldn't rely on the underlying

00:23:25.480 --> 00:23:28.300
virtual machine service account
identity because of the way

00:23:28.300 --> 00:23:29.710
Kubernetes scheduling works.

00:23:29.710 --> 00:23:33.490
Your workload could end up
scheduled on any given node,

00:23:33.490 --> 00:23:36.340
and in that case, you'd have
to grant too many permissions

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:39.430
to the underlying node.

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:43.000
Secondly, if a component that's
running Workload Identity

00:23:43.000 --> 00:23:44.920
gets compromised, we
need its blast radius

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.570
to be as small as possible.

00:23:47.570 --> 00:23:49.360
We primarily achieve
this by making sure

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:54.010
the long-lived durable secrets
that underpin this whole system

00:23:54.010 --> 00:23:56.290
are never exposed
to workloads at all,

00:23:56.290 --> 00:24:01.210
and the access tokens that we
do expose expire very quickly.

00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:02.910
And finally, I
wanted the solution

00:24:02.910 --> 00:24:04.970
to play nice with Kubernetes.

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:08.280
Kubernetes Engine customers
are probably our customers

00:24:08.280 --> 00:24:09.840
because they love Kubernetes.

00:24:09.840 --> 00:24:12.930
So we wanted to
design the solution

00:24:12.930 --> 00:24:15.240
in a way that fit in seamlessly
and followed the best

00:24:15.240 --> 00:24:17.640
practices and the way that
Kubernetes likes to do things.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:21.220
All right.

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:23.260
So if we're going to integrate
seamlessly with Kubernetes,

00:24:23.260 --> 00:24:24.770
and if we're going to
build this identity system,

00:24:24.770 --> 00:24:26.170
let's take a brief
aside and look

00:24:26.170 --> 00:24:27.378
at what we have to work with.

00:24:27.378 --> 00:24:29.498
This is like a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:24:29.498 --> 00:24:30.790
You've probably seen it before.

00:24:30.790 --> 00:24:31.900
It's got a control plane.

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:32.920
It's got nodes.

00:24:32.920 --> 00:24:34.140
Nodes have pods.

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.040
Your workload lives
in those pods.

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:39.218
Got a bunch of API objects.

00:24:39.218 --> 00:24:41.010
I didn't think we'd
have a screen this big,

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:42.650
so you probably can read them.

00:24:42.650 --> 00:24:44.780
On the left, we've
got a bunch that

00:24:44.780 --> 00:24:46.105
result in the creation of pods.

00:24:46.105 --> 00:24:47.480
And then on the
right, you've got

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:50.990
a bunch that provide various
controls for your cluster.

00:24:50.990 --> 00:24:53.150
The one that we actually
care about, in this case,

00:24:53.150 --> 00:24:55.130
is the service account.

00:24:55.130 --> 00:24:57.080
So the Kubernetes
service account

00:24:57.080 --> 00:25:00.140
is a really good junction
point for Kubernetes identities

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:01.950
and Google Cloud identities.

00:25:01.950 --> 00:25:03.410
You can create
them really easily.

00:25:03.410 --> 00:25:05.420
It's just a couple
of lines of YAML.

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:08.420
You can associate them
with a workload with one

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:10.603
line in your config file.

00:25:10.603 --> 00:25:12.020
And I think, most
importantly, you

00:25:12.020 --> 00:25:14.510
can set them up as the default
for an entire Kubernetes

00:25:14.510 --> 00:25:16.150
namespace.

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:19.188
That can be really useful if
you have a complicated or large

00:25:19.188 --> 00:25:20.230
infrastructure to manage.

00:25:22.627 --> 00:25:24.960
Except, your environment
probably didn't look like that.

00:25:24.960 --> 00:25:27.570
I am assuming that most
environments look more

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:28.420
like this.

00:25:28.420 --> 00:25:30.190
We've got multiple clusters.

00:25:30.190 --> 00:25:31.440
They're talking to each other.

00:25:31.440 --> 00:25:33.150
They're talking to Google Cloud.

00:25:33.150 --> 00:25:36.450
They're talking to
multiple cloud providers.

00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:38.310
And we wanted the
solution that we

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:42.490
build to be something that works
for the real world, for you.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:46.870
So enter the identity namespace.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:49.570
And this is my perfectly
accurate and entirely

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:51.700
incomprehensible definition.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:53.710
Jonathan stole my
other definition slide,

00:25:53.710 --> 00:25:55.130
so I'm keeping this one.

00:25:55.130 --> 00:25:57.400
I know it's usually easier
to go through these things

00:25:57.400 --> 00:26:01.330
by explaining what they do
rather than what they are,

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:03.990
but we're going to
use this one anyway.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:07.660
An identity namespace is a
scope in which a name uniquely

00:26:07.660 --> 00:26:09.880
identifies a principle.

00:26:09.880 --> 00:26:11.290
And what I mean
by scope is we're

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.660
going to be using this
identity namespace to define

00:26:13.660 --> 00:26:15.640
a collection of other things.

00:26:15.640 --> 00:26:19.600
And within that collection,
anything with the same name

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:21.680
is actually the same thing.

00:26:21.680 --> 00:26:23.920
So you can imagine it's
like a magic room where

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:28.182
everybody named Alice is
actually the same person.

00:26:28.182 --> 00:26:30.140
It's kind of a strange
concept in the abstract,

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:34.207
but it has some really useful
principles that we'll get into.

00:26:34.207 --> 00:26:36.040
And this is what it
looks like in real life.

00:26:36.040 --> 00:26:41.010
So when you enable Workload
Identity on your cluster,

00:26:41.010 --> 00:26:44.870
your Google Cloud project
gets an identity namespace.

00:26:44.870 --> 00:26:51.250
And within that namespace, you
can identify your Kubernetes

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.220
service accounts by a
combination of their Kubernetes

00:26:54.220 --> 00:26:58.020
namespace and
service account name.

00:26:58.020 --> 00:27:01.380
Because the combination of a
name and its identity namespace

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:04.770
is globally unique, and because
the things that we're naming

00:27:04.770 --> 00:27:06.810
are Kubernetes
service accounts, this

00:27:06.810 --> 00:27:09.230
means we can treat Alice
in cluster A and Alice

00:27:09.230 --> 00:27:11.552
in cluster B as the same person.

00:27:11.552 --> 00:27:13.260
And for all the crypto
nerds in the room,

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:16.780
there is no Bob or Eve
in this story at all.

00:27:16.780 --> 00:27:19.050
All right, so consider
this simplified example.

00:27:19.050 --> 00:27:20.780
We've got two
Kubernetes clusters.

00:27:20.780 --> 00:27:21.950
Each have two pods.

00:27:21.950 --> 00:27:24.648
Each have two namespaces.

00:27:24.648 --> 00:27:25.940
Each have two service accounts.

00:27:25.940 --> 00:27:27.830
They're completely independent
from an authorization

00:27:27.830 --> 00:27:28.530
standpoint.

00:27:28.530 --> 00:27:30.770
The identities that
exist in these clusters

00:27:30.770 --> 00:27:34.240
are only relevant
inside these clusters.

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:37.950
But if we bind both of these
to the same identity namespace,

00:27:37.950 --> 00:27:41.137
then we can start thinking
about logging robot--

00:27:41.137 --> 00:27:42.720
from an external
context, we can start

00:27:42.720 --> 00:27:45.600
thinking about logging robot
as the same in both Kubernetes

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:48.348
cluster 1 and
Kubernetes cluster 2.

00:27:48.348 --> 00:27:49.890
If you toss in a
little Google magic,

00:27:49.890 --> 00:27:52.265
we can then associate logging
robot with a Google service

00:27:52.265 --> 00:27:54.253
account and use it to
access cloud services.

00:27:57.500 --> 00:27:58.017
All right.

00:27:58.017 --> 00:27:59.600
So I'm going to
assume, at this point,

00:27:59.600 --> 00:28:02.610
the audience is relatively split
down the middle between the

00:28:02.610 --> 00:28:06.230
how does that work group and
the show me how to use it group.

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:08.450
So next up, Mike's
going to come up here

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:11.810
and demo how it all works in
practice with a live example,

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.225
assuming the demo is working.

00:28:13.225 --> 00:28:14.600
And then I'll come
back up and we

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.225
can do an architectural
lightning round

00:28:16.225 --> 00:28:18.680
for all the architecture nerds.

00:28:18.680 --> 00:28:20.105
And that's it.

00:28:20.105 --> 00:28:20.980
MICHAEL DANESE: Cool.

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:21.380
I have a mic, yeah.

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:22.713
AARON SMALL: Oh, you have a mic.

00:28:24.690 --> 00:28:25.940
MICHAEL DANESE: Oh, wait.

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:27.350
One more slide.

00:28:27.350 --> 00:28:30.380
Thank you.

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.885
This is my example app.

00:28:32.885 --> 00:28:34.760
It's actually a little
bit faded than it was,

00:28:34.760 --> 00:28:38.880
but there are a
couple more boxes.

00:28:38.880 --> 00:28:42.420
So in GKE US-West, there's a
box around the entire thing,

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:48.360
and that represents my GKE
cluster running in US West.

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:51.950
There's another box around it,
fuzzy llama and GCS server.

00:28:51.950 --> 00:28:56.000
So I have this app running
in the fuzzy llama namespace

00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:58.760
running as the GCS
server service account.

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:03.650
I used the GCP project name
generator to get fuzzy llama.

00:29:03.650 --> 00:29:05.720
And then I have
another GKE cluster

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:08.600
running in an eastern zone.

00:29:08.600 --> 00:29:13.190
And it has a namespace fuzzy
llama and my GCS server

00:29:13.190 --> 00:29:14.640
app running inside of it.

00:29:14.640 --> 00:29:18.080
And this is all running in
my project mikedanese-k8s.

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:24.800
So what these apps do is
they read a object from GCS

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:27.315
and serve it to my
customers, so it's

00:29:27.315 --> 00:29:29.000
a pretty sophisticated
