00:29:31.590 --> 00:29:33.600
So you can switch to
the other screen now.

00:29:36.640 --> 00:29:39.370
Awesome.

00:29:39.370 --> 00:29:41.240
Let's get started.

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:45.410
First thing I'm going to do is
I'm going to deploy a cluster.

00:29:45.410 --> 00:29:47.590
How's the size for everybody?

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:50.500
Would you like it a
little bit bigger?

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.390
All right, thank you.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:58.390
So I'm deploying our
cluster to us-west1-b.

00:29:58.390 --> 00:30:00.790
If you'll notice, I'm
passing this flag,

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:04.430
identity namespace
equals mikedanese-k8s,

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:08.807
so this is my project-wide
identity namespace.

00:30:08.807 --> 00:30:10.390
So I actually
pre-created this cluster

00:30:10.390 --> 00:30:13.150
because it's a bit slow.

00:30:13.150 --> 00:30:15.100
And the next thing
I'm going to do

00:30:15.100 --> 00:30:18.140
is I'm going to show you my app.

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:20.380
It's a pretty concise app.

00:30:20.380 --> 00:30:22.590
The yellow is the
important part.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:27.190
So I'm using Google's
Cloud SDK, just the default

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:31.990
client libraries to
construct a token source.

00:30:31.990 --> 00:30:34.990
If you are familiar with
how the token source works

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:36.790
in Compute Engine, it
just reads the token

00:30:36.790 --> 00:30:38.430
from the metadata server.

00:30:38.430 --> 00:30:43.840
I then grab an oauth client
that uses that token source,

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:50.720
and then I read one
object from GCS.

00:30:50.720 --> 00:30:55.210
So if we hop over to
my Kubernetes config,

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:58.960
all I really have is a service.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:02.780
It's a load balance service
that is serving on port 80

00:31:02.780 --> 00:31:06.080
to expose my app
to the customer.

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:10.430
I have a service account
named GCS server running

00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:13.010
in the fuzzy llama namespace.

00:31:13.010 --> 00:31:16.530
And I have an annotation on
that service account that says,

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:21.380
I want to run as the GCS
server GCP IAM service account,

00:31:21.380 --> 00:31:24.860
so this service account
has not been created yet.

00:31:24.860 --> 00:31:27.860
And then, at the very
bottom, I have my deployment

00:31:27.860 --> 00:31:33.140
that runs the app that we just
looked at as my GCS server

00:31:33.140 --> 00:31:35.280
Kubernetes service account.

00:31:35.280 --> 00:31:40.683
So pretty straightforward, let's
see if we can get it running.

00:31:40.683 --> 00:31:42.100
So the first thing
I'm going to do

00:31:42.100 --> 00:31:46.350
is I'm going to create that
GCS server service account,

00:31:46.350 --> 00:31:49.275
and I'm going to grab
credentials for my cluster.

00:31:52.680 --> 00:31:58.270
And apply that
application config,

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:01.240
and then make sure that
that app is coming up.

00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:04.160
So give it a couple seconds.

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:08.900
Looks like the GCS
server app is deployed.

00:32:08.900 --> 00:32:11.330
And the next thing
I'm going to do

00:32:11.330 --> 00:32:15.230
is I'm going to give my
Kubernetes service account

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:23.220
permission to run as the GCS
server IAM service account.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:26.420
So this is a bit dense,
so I'll unpack it.

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:30.980
We are adding an IAM
policy that grants

00:32:30.980 --> 00:32:35.030
GCS server in the
fuzzy llama namespace

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:42.290
and the mikedanese-k8s identity
namespace token creator

00:32:42.290 --> 00:32:45.530
permission on the
IAM service account

00:32:45.530 --> 00:32:47.250
that we want that app to run as.

00:32:49.940 --> 00:32:53.150
So this actually
is the step that

00:32:53.150 --> 00:32:55.520
grants my Kubernetes
service account permission

00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:59.190
to use the IAM service account.

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:02.005
So that looks good.

00:33:02.005 --> 00:33:03.380
Next thing I'm
going to do is I'm

00:33:03.380 --> 00:33:07.370
going to pull that app
in my west cluster,

00:33:07.370 --> 00:33:10.760
and I'm getting 403 forbidden.

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:15.050
Well, that's probably because
we haven't given my application

00:33:15.050 --> 00:33:19.850
permission to read that object.

00:33:19.850 --> 00:33:21.245
So the next thing
I'm going to do

00:33:21.245 --> 00:33:23.800
is going to be to
use gsutil change

00:33:23.800 --> 00:33:29.570
acl to grant that GCP service
account permission to read

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:32.540
that image or that object.

00:33:32.540 --> 00:33:35.645
And we get kind
of what we expect.

00:33:35.645 --> 00:33:37.270
I also noticed-- this
will be important

00:33:37.270 --> 00:33:39.103
when we set up the
second half of the demo--

00:33:39.103 --> 00:33:44.750
that the cluster name is
exposed as a red header.

00:33:44.750 --> 00:33:51.350
So let's go set up the
second half of the demo.

00:33:51.350 --> 00:33:53.870
So I'm going to do
the exact same thing.

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:58.810
The cluster is called east, but
it's deployed in us-central.

00:33:58.810 --> 00:34:01.255
I actually also
pre-created this cluster.

00:34:01.255 --> 00:34:02.630
So the next thing
I'm going to do

00:34:02.630 --> 00:34:06.170
is I'm going to grab
credentials for it,

00:34:06.170 --> 00:34:10.880
and then I'm going to apply
the same application config.

00:34:10.880 --> 00:34:23.030
And make sure it's
running, looks pretty good.

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:25.090
And then what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:34:25.090 --> 00:34:29.810
going to pull east and
west at the same time.

00:34:29.810 --> 00:34:32.020
So as you can see,
this one hit west.

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:33.280
That one hit east.

00:34:33.280 --> 00:34:41.139
So both my applications are
able to read that GCS object.

00:34:41.139 --> 00:34:44.230
The reason we didn't have
to add any acls to set up

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:47.110
the eastern cluster is
because both clusters are

00:34:47.110 --> 00:34:51.179
in the same identity namespace,
so Google authenticates them

00:34:51.179 --> 00:34:52.685
as the same service account.

00:34:56.489 --> 00:35:00.300
So the last thing that
we will do in this demo

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:05.820
is I'm going to revoke acl just
to bring that principle home.

00:35:05.820 --> 00:35:11.580
This acl revoke will apply to
both clusters simultaneously.

00:35:11.580 --> 00:35:17.160
So west just failed,
and east just failed.

00:35:17.160 --> 00:35:21.300
So west and east are
running the same service.

00:35:21.300 --> 00:35:25.480
My GCS server application
spans both those clusters,

00:35:25.480 --> 00:35:27.720
and that's the demo.

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:29.020
Thank you.

00:35:29.020 --> 00:35:32.415
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:42.703 --> 00:35:45.120
AARON SMALL: OK, I promised
you an architectural lightning

00:35:45.120 --> 00:35:45.870
round.

00:35:45.870 --> 00:35:48.840
For those of you who kept your
hands up in the beginning,

00:35:48.840 --> 00:35:51.870
this bit's for you.

00:35:51.870 --> 00:35:54.990
There are five
fundamental new components

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:57.180
that exist as a part
of this new feature.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.550
First, we have an OIDC
provider that runs

00:35:59.550 --> 00:36:01.200
on Google's infrastructure.

00:36:01.200 --> 00:36:06.820
It provides cluster-specific
metadata and it signs JWTs.

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:09.630
There is a new GKE
metadata server.

00:36:09.630 --> 00:36:14.420
It runs as a DaemonSet, meaning
one instance on every node.

00:36:14.420 --> 00:36:17.660
It emulates the existing
VMs metadata server,

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:19.950
but it exposes
pod-specific data.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:21.950
This is going to provide
backwards compatibility

00:36:21.950 --> 00:36:26.060
with old client libraries.

00:36:26.060 --> 00:36:28.665
And it also offers like
built-in metadata concealment.

00:36:28.665 --> 00:36:31.040
I don't know if any of you
are familiar with that feature

00:36:31.040 --> 00:36:32.460
that's in beta today on GKE.

00:36:32.460 --> 00:36:36.587
It isolates some secrets that
exist in the GCE metadata

00:36:36.587 --> 00:36:38.420
server from being exposed
to your workloads,

00:36:38.420 --> 00:36:43.210
and this is built into this
new metadata server as well.

00:36:43.210 --> 00:36:46.320
We also have a new
annotation that you

00:36:46.320 --> 00:36:48.205
saw earlier on the
service account which

00:36:48.205 --> 00:36:51.090
lets you indicate which
GCP service account

00:36:51.090 --> 00:36:52.863
you want to act as.

00:36:52.863 --> 00:36:54.530
That's not actually
a security boundary,

00:36:54.530 --> 00:36:57.140
but we do need it
to provide the hint.

00:36:57.140 --> 00:36:58.810
And then, of course,
we have Google IAM.

00:36:58.810 --> 00:36:59.990
It issues access tokens.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:01.220
It validates bindings.

00:37:01.220 --> 00:37:04.520
The new thing is it also
validates OIDC signatures now.

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:08.280
And then Google Cloud, at
large, it accepts access tokens

00:37:08.280 --> 00:37:11.892
and it does pretty
much anything.

00:37:11.892 --> 00:37:13.600
We're going to walk
through the flow now.

00:37:13.600 --> 00:37:15.680
There's two steps
that are left out,

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:19.120
and that's an authorized
user binding the cluster

00:37:19.120 --> 00:37:19.900
to the namespace.

00:37:19.900 --> 00:37:21.720
You saw Mike do that earlier.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:24.850
And the second is the workload
needs to try and do something

00:37:24.850 --> 00:37:28.270
to try and access a Google Cloud
Service using a client library.

00:37:28.270 --> 00:37:30.970
Once that happens, the
GKE metadata server

00:37:30.970 --> 00:37:33.400
is going to request
an OIDC signed JWT

00:37:33.400 --> 00:37:37.670
from the control plane.

00:37:37.670 --> 00:37:38.280
Oh, I'm sorry.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:41.140
I should note, that
connection is authenticated

00:37:41.140 --> 00:37:44.098
using mutual TLS, and it
uses the node's identity

00:37:44.098 --> 00:37:44.890
as the client cert.

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:49.740
Then the GKE metadata
server is going

00:37:49.740 --> 00:37:53.070
to use that OIDC signed
JWT to request an access

00:37:53.070 --> 00:37:56.400
token for the identity namespace
slash Kubernetes service

00:37:56.400 --> 00:37:59.070
account from IAM.

00:37:59.070 --> 00:38:03.430
IAM is going to validate that
the appropriate bindings exist,

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:05.530
and then it's going
to validate that it

00:38:05.530 --> 00:38:08.700
was signed by the cluster's
correct OIDC provider.

00:38:08.700 --> 00:38:10.450
It's then going to
return an access token.

00:38:10.450 --> 00:38:11.908
And this access
token, remember, is

00:38:11.908 --> 00:38:16.655
for the identity namespace slash
Kubernetes service account.

00:38:16.655 --> 00:38:18.030
Then the metadata
server is going

00:38:18.030 --> 00:38:22.390
to send that access token
it just got back to IAM,

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:25.330
and IAM is going to exchange
that for a short-lived GCP

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:27.760
service account token
after validating

00:38:27.760 --> 00:38:30.120
the appropriate
bindings, of course.

00:38:30.120 --> 00:38:33.840
Then the GKE metadata
server returns that token up

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:35.670
to the workload,
and the workload

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:40.380
can obviously use that to
make calls to any GCP service.

00:38:40.380 --> 00:38:43.560
There's a lot of token exchanges
and validations going on.

00:38:43.560 --> 00:38:45.960
But in practice,
what this means is,

00:38:45.960 --> 00:38:47.430
once you've done
the configuration

00:38:47.430 --> 00:38:52.480
and set up your
identity namespace,

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:55.568
your identity in Google Cloud
is determined by the Kubernetes

00:38:55.568 --> 00:38:57.610
service account that your
workload is running as,

00:38:57.610 --> 00:38:59.660
and everything else just works.

00:38:59.660 --> 00:39:03.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

